# Commits that touched a great many lines without changing what any of them
# does. `git blame` skips them and reports the commit underneath, which is the
# one that actually wrote the code.
#
# Enable it once per clone:
#
#     git config blame.ignoreRevsFile .git-blame-ignore-revs
#
# It cannot be enabled from inside the repository: blame.ignoreRevsFile is a
# configuration setting, and git does not read configuration out of a working
# tree it just cloned. GitHub, GitLab and Gitea read this file without being
# asked.
#
# Adding an entry is a two-step sequence, because a commit cannot contain its
# own hash:
#
#   1. Make the mass change and commit it on its own, touching nothing else.
#   2. Append the hash that commit was given, with the subject after it, and
#      commit that one line separately. The second commit is one line long, so
#      it never needs ignoring itself.
#
# Only commits that rewrote lines which still exist belong here. A commit that
# deleted lines moves no blame, and a mechanical rename carrying real changes
# alongside it would hide genuine authorship, so neither is listed.

